  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_da46a453",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a historical battle scene with soldiers engaged in combat, overlaid with the text: 'when you go out to fight for independence but forgot to lock up the slaves.' This juxtaposition uses a historical event (likely the American Revolution or a similar independence struggle) to imply that the fight for freedom was hypocritical or incomplete because enslaved people were not liberated. The meme's intent is to critique or mock the historical failure to extend freedom to all people, particularly Black people, who were enslaved. While not explicitly targeting a specific individual, it relies on a racialized historical narrative that can be interpreted as harmful by implying that the struggle for independence was inherently flawed or incomplete without including Black people. The meme may be seen as perpetuating a stereotype that the fight for freedom was 'incomplete' without addressing the enslavement of Black people, which can be interpreted as a form of historical revisionism that minimizes the suffering of enslaved people and the moral complexity of the independence movements. The meme's intent is to provoke thought or criticism about historical hypocrisy, but its framing can be seen as harmful because it reduces a complex historical struggle to a simplistic, potentially offensive, and racially charged analogy.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d2df8b15"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
