      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b524804c",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "The text 'slaves' is mapped onto the soldiers in the battle scene.",
      "description": "The textual phrase 'forgot to lock up the slaves' is juxtaposed with the visual depiction of soldiers in combat. The meme implies that the soldiers (representing the colonists fighting for independence) are the ones who 'forgot' to free the enslaved people, who are not visually depicted as distinct individuals in the image. This creates an analogical mapping where the soldiers are implicitly mapped as the ones who 'forgot' to free the slaves, even though the slaves themselves are not visually present. The mapping is based on the historical context that the soldiers (colonists) were often slaveholders, and the text critiques this hypocrisy by suggesting the soldiers failed to extend freedom to those they enslaved. The soldiers are the visual entity being mapped onto the textual concept of 'slaves' as the group that was not freed.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d2df8b15"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_f3b94775",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the textual material",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the text chunk 'when you go out to fight for independence but forgot to lock up the slaves' because the phrase uses the second-person pronoun 'you', directly addressing the viewer. This creates an implicit identification where the user is positioned as the 'you' in the scenario — the person who 'forgot to lock up the slaves' — thereby projecting the user's identity onto the textual material. The user is not projected onto the visual material or scene directly, but the text serves as the primary locus of this projection due to its deictic reference.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d2df8b15"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_5d342c84",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme uses a racially charged historical analogy to imply that the fight for independence was hypocritical or incomplete because enslaved people were not liberated, which can be interpreted as harmful by minimizing the suffering of enslaved people and reducing a complex historical struggle to a simplistic, potentially offensive, and racially charged analogy.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d2df8b15"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_03aae1ba",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "audience description",
      "description": "The target community for this meme is individuals who are familiar with or interested in American Revolutionary history, particularly those who understand the historical context of slavery in the American colonies. The meme is designed to provoke thought or criticism about historical hypocrisy, specifically targeting audiences who engage with or are aware of the moral contradictions in the fight for independence while maintaining slavery. It relies on a racialized historical narrative that may resonate with audiences who have been exposed to or are critical of the legacy of American founding figures and the exclusion of Black people from the narrative of freedom. The meme's intent to critique historical failure to extend freedom to all people, particularly Black people, suggests it is aimed at an audience that is culturally aware of racial dynamics in American history and may be receptive to or engaged with discussions about historical revisionism and racialized narratives.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_d2df8b15"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    }
